## The 2ct Milestone

The 2ct Milestone is the concept that defines this size category. It describes the price cliff natural asschers cross at 2ct — and why lab becomes the dominant choice for most buyers here.

No other asscher size has a gap this wide between lab and natural equivalents. At 1ct, the gap is meaningful. At 2ct, it is transformational.

Same carat weight. Same shape. Completely different price.

The [IGI 2.01ct D-FL at $5,536](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28794228?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and a GIA 2.01ct G-IF natural at $25,053 are both 2.01ct asscher cuts. The natural costs $19,517 more.

The lab stone is D-FL — Flawless clarity, D color. The natural stone is G-IF — also Internally Flawless, but G color. The lab stone has better color and costs $19,517 less.

That is The 2ct Milestone.

The reason is scarcity. Natural 2ct+ diamonds require clean rough at a size that does not occur frequently in diamond-bearing rock. Cutting a 2ct finished stone requires approximately 4ct of rough minimum.

Clean 4ct rough in asscher-compatible octahedral form is rare. Lab growing has no such constraint — the process is controlled and the output is consistent.

This is not a minor quality difference. The lab option in this guide is D color and FL clarity — better grades than most natural stones at any price. The scarcity premium you pay for natural goes toward origin, not toward a better stone on the grade scale.

> "Every buyer I work with at the 2ct size has the same moment. They see the lab price. They see the natural price. They ask me if there is something wrong with the lab stone. There is nothing wrong with it. D-FL is the highest possible grade. The asscher's step facets will show you that Flawless clarity in every reflection. The only difference is that the natural stone formed underground and the lab stone did not." — Farzana Hasan, **[Diamond Critics](https://diamondcritics.com/about-farzana/)**

