[![Asscher cut diamond ring rose gold three-stone model pavé band on hand](/images/asscher-cut-diamond-three-stone-ring-rose-gold-model.jpg)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/asscher-cut-diamond-rings?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

### What to Check on a Natural 2ct Asscher GIA Certificate

At $21,000+ for the stone, the GIA certificate is not the whole answer. Request the inclusion plot.

Check inclusion position first. A VS1 inclusion at the girdle edge of a 2ct asscher is essentially invisible face-up. The same VS1 inclusion centered under the table is visible in indoor light.

The plot shows exactly where each inclusion sits — the grade alone does not tell you this.

Check table percentage next: 58–65% is the target for asscher cuts. Outside this range the windmill effect is incomplete — the stone looks flat or too deep. Always request a face-up video before committing to any natural 2ct asscher.

At this price, you are entitled to see the stone before you buy it.

> "I will not recommend a natural 2ct asscher without a face-up video. The certificate tells you the grade. The video tells you what you are actually buying. At $21,000 for a stone, any retailer who will not provide a face-up video is a retailer to walk away from." — Farzana Hasan, **[Diamond Critics](https://diamondcritics.com/about-farzana/)**

